    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d307">

                <pb facs="0737" n="717" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v02/pg717"/>

                <head>307. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Embassy in Vietnam<anchor n="1" xml:id="d307-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1962-10-22T16:06:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 22,
                                1962—4:06 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>466. For Ambassador Nolting from
                        Harriman. Your 443<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d307-f2"/> indicates that you and I are not on the same wavelength.
                    The Presidentʼs position is already doubly engaged, not only by his personal
                    letter to Diem,<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d307-f3"/> but also by Laos settlement which must be made to achieve
                    US objectives as far as possible. In making settlement we have made it plain to
                    all concerned that we consider it not as ending all conflict, but transferring
                    conflict from military to political area. In this conflict we have a right to
                    expect full support and assistance from GVN as
                    ally as well as a signatory. From your messages I gained the impression that you
                    do not consider Diemʼs attitude towards
                    Laos of prime importance.</p>
                <pb facs="0738" n="718" xml:id="pg718"/>
                <p>What happens in Laos will have substantial influence on South Viet-Nam and
                    therefore I have asked you to make Diem
                    understand the importance we place upon it and the fact that we have a right to
                    ask him as an ally to cooperate in matters which so vitally affect our efforts
                    to keep South Viet-Nam free. This is also true of Cambodia where GVN is continuing to aggravate situation to our
                    mutual disadvantage.</p>
                <p>I am relieved to learn from your 449 that Diem is beginning to recognize his obligations toward the
                        President.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d307-f4"/> If, however, this does not occur,
                    your are instructed to make another approach along lines of Deptel 459 or in whatever manner in your
                    judgment you consider most appropriate. We have never suggested application
                    sanctions, and you are not to give any implication that US has any idea of
                    applying them. When you talk to Diem you
                    should talk as an ally to another expecting support due us and may inform him
                    you are speaking under instructions.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Rusk</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
